\subsection{Function-Level Formulation Comparison}
\label{sec:equivalence}

\cls{} matches \cwm{} within $\pm$2\pp{} (TOST $p = 0.007$, observed gap 1.24\pp{}, 5 seeds; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}); the minimum equivalence margin is $\pm$1.63\pp{}. \cls{} shows a small advantage over \traj{} ($+$1.89\pp{} mean across 3 paired seeds, df${=}$2; TOST $p = 0.230$ at $\pm$2\pp{}, $p = 0.020$ at $\pm$2.5\pp{}; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:tost_cls_traj}). Among the three formulations, \cls{} achieves the best or comparable accuracy across all function-level settings. Within-problem predictions are correlated (ICC $= 0.191$, $p < 0.0001$, design effect (DEFF) $= 3.97$); all statistical tests use clustered bootstrap accordingly. On DeepSeek-Coder, the observed \cls{}--\cwm{} gap of 0.02\pp{} is consistent with equivalence, but with only 2 seeds the comparison is underpowered (TOST not significant even at $\pm$5\pp{} margin). Per-test classification provides diagnostic information (which specific tests fail) at comparable or better accuracy, paralleling process supervision~\cite{lightman2023letsverify} where step-level verification enables error localization.

The \cls{}--\cwm{} equivalence is margin-sensitive: at $\pm$1\pp{}, the observed gap prevents rejection ($p = 0.871$); our $\pm$2\pp{} margin reflects the minimal clinically meaningful difference for verification accuracy (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}). Oracle analysis on SWE-bench Verified (500 instances) shows that per-test advantage concentrates at $T \geq 2$ fail-to-pass tests (31.3\% of instances, contributing 55.3\% of the total oracle advantage; Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}).

