\subsection{Per-Test vs.\ Trajectory-Level Equivalence}
\label{sec:equivalence}

We compare \cls{} (aggregated via all-pass rule) against \traj{} on 523 variant-level instances across five seeds. The pooled gap is $-$0.45\pp{} (95\% CI [$-$1.71, +0.84]); the TOST equivalence test confirms statistical equivalence at $p = 0.007$ ($\pm$2\pp{} margin), with a minimum equivalence margin of $\pm$1.63\pp{}. The $\pm$2\pp{} margin reflects practical significance: formulation differences below 2\pp{} are unlikely to affect deployment decisions where absolute accuracy dominates the cost-benefit analysis. Within-problem predictions are correlated (intraclass correlation ICC $= 0.191$, $p < 0.0001$, design effect (DEFF) $= 3.97$); all statistical tests use clustered bootstrap accordingly. On DeepSeek-Coder, the observed \cls{}--\cwm{} gap of 0.02\pp{} is consistent with equivalence, but with only 2 seeds the comparison is underpowered (TOST not significant even at $\pm$5\pp{} margin). Per-test classification provides diagnostic information (which specific tests fail) at no cost to variant-level accuracy, a result that parallels process supervision~\cite{lightman2023letsverify}, where step-level verification enables error localization without accuracy cost.

Equivalence is margin-sensitive: at $\pm$1\pp{}, the null hypothesis of difference cannot be rejected ($p = 0.871$); our $\pm$2\pp{} margin reflects the minimal clinically meaningful difference for verification accuracy. Full sensitivity analysis appears in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}. Oracle analysis on SWE-bench Verified (500 instances) shows that per-test advantage concentrates at $T \geq 2$ fail-to-pass tests (31.3\% of instances, contributing 55.3\% of the total oracle advantage; Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}). \cls{} achieves slightly higher accuracy than \traj{} across both model families (gap 2.1--2.7\pp{}).

